meta { title = Functions and pipes, order = 3, group = Learn }

Declare a function with fn, followed by its name and parameters. Call it by writing the function and its arguments separated by spaces. Parentheses group expressions; they are also useful when a call is an argument to another call.

h2 { Compose calls }

A pipe written as -> passes a value to the next function. In this example, 10 flows through double and then addThree. The result is 23.

A function can return another function. addOffset returns add, which remembers offset from its enclosing call. These ordinary closures run in the current execution environment; they cannot be transferred directly to a parallel worker.

{{example:functions}}

h2 { Keep grouping explicit }

Use parentheses when combining calls, arithmetic, or comparisons if you are uncertain about grouping. Some historical expression-binding behavior is deliberately preserved while the port is completed.

